<block
  xmlns="http://blockml.org/schema/block"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://blockml.org/schema/block https://blockml.org/schemas/core/block.xsd
                      "
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation">
  <name>CompilerArchitecture</name>
  <type>doc:CompilerArchitecture</type>
  <baseType>core:Documentation</baseType>
  <is>A guide to the compiler pipeline</is>

  <documentation>
    The BOM Compiler follows a three-layer architecture with immutable Canonical BOM at the center:

    BML → Parser (@blockml/parser) → Canonical BOM (immutable, lossless SSOT)

    Framework Utilities (@blockml/core) — optional, composable; never mutate Canonical BOM:
    resolveInheritance, resolveEffectiveMembers, parseExpression, expandComposition,
    validateTypes, validateConstraints, …

    Renderer Pipeline (target-specific) — orchestrates utilities, owns working model, emits output.

    Validation pipeline — three independent stages:
    Stage 1 — XML (Parser): syntax, namespaces, XSD. No semantics.
    Stage 2 — Validator: inheritance, contracts, types, constraints, references,
              expression correctness. Expressions are NOT evaluated.
    Stage 3 — Renderer: expression evaluation, optional handling, capability interpretation,
              code generation, runtime behaviour.

    The parser makes no semantic decisions. It parses XML, resolves namespaces and type references
    syntactically, and transfers the BML tree into a canonical object structure. All BML information
    must be fully preserved. Resolved inheritance, effective properties, expanded composition, and
    evaluated expressions must never be written back into the Canonical BOM.

    Self-hosting bootstrap: before processing user projects, the CLI bootstraps a TypeRegistry
    from framework BML in @blockml/core and domain packages.
  </documentation>

  <references>
    <see file="06_compiler.md">
